WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.262
Hi, I'm Pixel!

00:00:02.262 --> 00:00:06.471
Voxel Musou pits one hero against a voxel army in a browser tab.

00:00:06.471 --> 00:00:07.119
Here's how.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:12.895
The trick: the army is a fixed pool of three hundred slots.

00:00:12.895 --> 00:00:16.482
A director keeps about eighty-four on the hero, and recycles the dead.

00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:22.217
Every soldier is a slot in a pool, sized once at boot.

00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:23.727
Nothing is allocated after that.

00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:27.132
Every twenty frames, a director counts the fighters.

00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:30.118
Under eighty-four, the nearest waiting block marches in.

00:00:30.538 --> 00:00:34.248
A knocked-out soldier frees his slot three and a half seconds later,

00:00:34.248 --> 00:00:35.794
and returns as a reinforcement.

00:00:36.214 --> 00:00:39.729
So the enemies setting only changes how many wait at the edge.

00:00:39.729 --> 00:00:41.194
The fight stays eighty-four strong.

00:00:42.525 --> 00:00:45.778
Each frame banks its elapsed time, capped at a tenth of a second.

00:00:46.198 --> 00:00:50.198
It spends that time in sixtieth-of-a-second steps, at most four per frame.

00:00:50.618 --> 00:00:53.882
If it's still behind, it drops the rest, so a slow machine

00:00:53.882 --> 00:00:54.970
plays in slow motion.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:56.445
Then it draws.

00:00:56.445 --> 00:00:58.905
Two seeded random streams make fights repeatable.

00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:03.667
Each hit freezes the hero for a few frames, which would drag

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.080
the combo off its beat.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.211
One rule fixes that.

00:01:06.631 --> 00:01:08.638
Move T: frames the move has played.

00:01:09.678 --> 00:01:11.818
Stop: frames spent frozen by hitstop.

00:01:12.919 --> 00:01:16.204
Absorb is eight: up to eight frozen frames count as played.

00:01:16.624 --> 00:01:19.705
So the next strike starts on time, even in a ring of twenty.

00:01:21.092 --> 00:01:25.881
From the move table: strikes twenty-five, twenty-six, twenty-four and twenty-six frames apart,

00:01:25.881 --> 00:01:27.477
then a late thirty-five.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.940
The feel is data: a pool, a clock and a frame table

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.228
you can check by hand.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.566
To recap: a recycled pool, a clock that slows instead of spiralling,

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:42.524
and hitstop folded into the beat.

00:01:42.944 --> 00:01:45.080
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:45.690
I'm Pixel.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:45.995
Bye!
